.TH KIRO-AUR-CHECK-NOTICE 8 "2026-11-26" "Kiro" "Kiro System Tools"
.SH NAME
kiro-aur-check-notice \- tell the user that the AUR check exists and how to switch it on
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kiro-aur-check-notice
[\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR]
[\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kiro-aur-check-notice
runs from the pacman hook
.I /etc/pacman.d/hooks/zz-kiro-aur-check-notice.hook
when paru or yay is installed or updated, and once when kiro-system-files first installs it.
For the user who ran pacman (\fBSUDO_USER\fR) it asks
.BR kiro-paru-aur-check (8)
and
.BR kiro-yay-aur-check (8)
whether the check is on, and for each installed helper where it was never set up it prints, in
colour, how to switch it on: ATT (Pacman page, AUR check) or one command. It does this once per
user: afterwards a stamp in
.I /var/lib/kiro/aur-check-notice
keeps it quiet. A check the user switched off (the Kiro marker with the line commented out, or
an own PreBuildCommand) counts as a choice and gets no notice. It never changes a config:
switching the check on is up to the user. Delete the stamp to see the notice again.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SUDO_USER
The user whose paru and yay configs are read. Without it (pacman run as root) only
.I /etc/paru.conf
is checked for paru, and yay is reported as off.
.TP
.B TERM ", " NO_COLOR
pacman pipes hook output, so the colours come from \fBTERM\fR; \fBNO_COLOR\fR or a dumb
terminal turns them off.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kiro-aur-check (8),
.BR kiro-paru-aur-check (8),
.BR kiro-yay-aur-check (8)
